  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:vG-NsFXNYUw",
    "purpose": "Shows how to break, peel, slice and mince fresh garlic, explains why cut garlic changes flavor, and runs a blind taste test of store-bought prepared garlic products against fresh garlic.",
    "video_type": "comparison",
    "thesis": "Garlic's flavor changes once its cells are ruptured, so fresh garlic that you peel and cut yourself is the benchmark against which supermarket garlic forms (pre-peeled, puree, chopped in water or oil, granulated) are judged in a blind taste test.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Rub off the papery skin, then press the head with your palm to separate the cloves.",
      "Shaking cloves between two bowls can peel them, but it depends on the age of the garlic.",
      "For a quick knife peel, cut the root end and pull to half-peel the clove; a light tap loosens the peel further if you will mince or smash it.",
      "The green germ can taste slightly bitter and is worth removing for raw uses like aioli or toum, less so when cooking.",
      "Garlic can be minced with a heavy Chinese cleaver (smash then chop), with a mortar and pestle, or pureed using coarse kosher salt as an abrasive.",
      "The more garlic cells are ruptured, and the longer the garlic sits after cutting, the stronger its pungency.",
      "Pre-peeled garlic is blanched to loosen the skins, so it can taste slightly cooked.",
      "In the blind test, several jarred products tasted strongly of citric acid, which is used as a preservative and texture control.",
      "Granulated garlic is treated as a different ingredient from fresh garlic, useful in rubs and ranch dressing; The Wok's gochujang ranch uses both."
    ],
    "outline": [
