      {
        "title": "Bowl-Shaking Garlic Trick",
        "summary": "Shaking cloves between two bowls to peel them, noting it works only depending on the garlic's age.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Fast Knife Peeling Method",
        "summary": "Cutting and pulling the root end to half-peel cloves, tapping to loosen peels, slicing pole to pole with a claw grip, and when to remove the green germ.",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Mincing Methods (Knife, Mortar, Salt)",
        "summary": "Mincing with a heavy Chinese cleaver, pounding in a mortar and pestle, how rupturing cells affects pungency, removing the germ with a paring knife, and pureeing with kosher salt.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Why Fresh Garlic Matters",
        "summary": "Explains that once garlic is cut its flavor starts to change irreversibly, so pre-cut forms taste different from fresh.",
        "first_segment": 9,
        "last_segment": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "Taste Test Setup",
        "summary": "Introduces Lena and the lineup: fresh-chopped garlic, garlic puree, chopped garlic in water, chopped garlic in olive oil, and pre-peeled cloves, with why blanching affects pre-peeled garlic.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 11
      },
      {
        "title": "Blind Garlic Taste Test",
        "summary": "Kenji sniffs and tastes six unlabeled garlic samples and describes each one's pungency, acidity, salt and texture.",
        "first_segment": 11,
        "last_segment": 14
      },
      {
        "title": "Ranking & Recap",
        "summary": "Groups the samples by sharpness and citric acid flavor, explains citric acid as a preservative, and discusses how cooking evens out differences.",
        "first_segment": 14,
        "last_segment": 16
      },
      {
        "title": "What Garlic to Use When",
        "summary": "Recommends an order of preference among the forms and explains granulated garlic as a distinct ingredient, citing the gochujang ranch recipe in The Wok.",
        "first_segment": 16,
